WEBVTT

00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:08.800
( squeaky smooching )

00:00:08.884 --> 00:00:10.927
Grandpa, don’t make it gross.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:14.264
Tell him about
the Indian prince.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.891
He’d like to
hear about that.

00:00:15.974 --> 00:00:18.935
You mean,
Prince Pondicherry.

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:20.437
Well,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.690
Prince Pondicherry wrote
a letter to Mr. Wonka

00:00:23.732 --> 00:00:26.401
and asked him to come
all the way out to India

00:00:26.443 --> 00:00:31.490
and build him a colossal palace,
entirely out of chocolate.

00:00:31.573 --> 00:00:33.742
( chuckles )

00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:35.785
WONKA:
It will have 100 rooms

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.412
and everything
will be made

00:00:37.496 --> 00:00:39.748
of either dark
or light chocolate.

00:00:42.918 --> 00:00:45.712
GRANDPA JOE:
True to his word,

00:00:45.754 --> 00:00:47.756
the bricks were chocolate

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:50.175
and the cement holding them
together was chocolate.

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:54.262
All the walls and ceilings were
made of chocolate, as well.

00:00:54.346 --> 00:00:59.184
So were the carpets and the
pictures and the furniture.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:01.561
It is perfect in every way.

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:03.772
Yeah, but it
won’t last long.

00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:05.148
You better start
eating right now.

00:01:05.232 --> 00:01:06.149
( scoffs )

00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:07.150
Nonsense.

00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:09.319
I will not eat my palace.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.113
I intend to live in it.

00:01:15.242 --> 00:01:18.245
GRANDPA JOE:
But Mr. Wonka was right,
of course.

00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:20.956
Soon after this, there came

00:01:20.997 --> 00:01:24.209
a very hot day
with a boiling sun.

00:01:24.292 --> 00:01:25.293
( inhales deeply )

00:01:25.377 --> 00:01:26.753
Mmm...

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.554
( laughs )

00:01:42.978 --> 00:01:44.020
Oh.

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:48.692
( rumbling )

00:01:59.578 --> 00:02:01.621
GRANDPA JOE:
The prince sent

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:04.833
an urgent telegram
requesting a new palace,

00:02:04.916 --> 00:02:07.919
but Willy Wonka was facing
problems of his own.

00:02:08.003 --> 00:02:11.089
All the other chocolate makers,

00:02:11.172 --> 00:02:14.509
you see, had grown jealous
of Mr. Wonka.

00:02:14.593 --> 00:02:19.306
They began sending in spies
to steal his secret recipes.

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:30.275
( light espionage-style
music playing )

00:02:46.458 --> 00:02:49.628
Fickelgruber started making
an ice cream

00:02:49.711 --> 00:02:51.212
that would never melt.

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:53.882
Prodnose came out
with a chewing gum

00:02:53.965 --> 00:02:55.884
that never lost its flavor.

00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:58.970
Then Slugworth began
making candy balloons

00:02:59.054 --> 00:03:02.265
that you could blow up
to incredible sizes.

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:03.308
Oh!

00:03:03.391 --> 00:03:04.517
The thievery got so bad

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:07.562
that, one day,
without a warning,

00:03:07.604 --> 00:03:12.067
Mr. Wonka told every single one
of his workers to go home.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:13.401
He announced

00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:17.447
that he was closing
his chocolate factory forever.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.660
WONKA:
I’m closing
my chocolate factory forever.

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:23.411
I’m sorry.

00:03:26.456 --> 00:03:27.707
( gates clang shut )

00:03:32.921 --> 00:03:34.172
( door thuds closed )

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.386
( utensils clinking )

00:03:39.427 --> 00:03:41.596
But it didn’t close forever.

00:03:41.638 --> 00:03:42.972
It’s open right now.

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:45.642
Ah, yes, well, sometimes
when grownups say "forever,"

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:47.560
they mean "a very long time."

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:48.895
GRANDPA GEORGE:
Such as "I feel like

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:51.439
I’ve eaten nothing but
cabbage soup forever."

00:03:51.523 --> 00:03:52.607
Now, Pops.

00:03:52.691 --> 00:03:55.110
The factory did close, Charlie.

00:03:55.151 --> 00:03:59.406
And it seemed like it was
going to be closed forever.

00:03:59.447 --> 00:04:02.450
Then, one day,
we saw smoke

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:03.785
rising from the chimneys.

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:06.663
The factory was
back in business.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.456
Did you get your job back?

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.625
No.

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:12.043
No one did.

00:04:15.422 --> 00:04:17.924
But there must be
people working there.

00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:19.467
Think about it, Charlie.

00:04:19.551 --> 00:04:21.678
Have you ever seen
a single person

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.471
going into that factory?

00:04:23.513 --> 00:04:24.764
Or coming out of it?

00:04:25.724 --> 00:04:26.975
No.

00:04:27.016 --> 00:04:28.768
The gates are
always closed.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.687
Exactly.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.565
But then, who’s running
the machines?

00:04:33.648 --> 00:04:34.941
Nobody knows, Charlie.

00:04:34.983 --> 00:04:36.651
It certainly is a mystery.

00:04:36.693 --> 00:04:38.945
Hasn’t someone asked Mr. Wonka?

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:42.198
Nobody sees him anymore.

00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:43.450
He never comes out.

00:04:43.491 --> 00:04:46.703
The only thing that comes
out of that place

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:50.540
is the candy, already
packed and addressed.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:56.296
I’d give anything in the world
just to go in one more time

00:04:56.337 --> 00:05:01.342
and see what’s become
of that amazing factory.

00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:02.844
Well, you won’t.

00:05:02.927 --> 00:05:04.471
Because you can’t.

00:05:04.512 --> 00:05:05.930
No one can.

00:05:06.014 --> 00:05:07.682
It’s a mystery

00:05:07.724 --> 00:05:10.018
and it will always be a mystery.

00:05:10.101 --> 00:05:12.312
That little factory of yours,
Charlie,

00:05:12.353 --> 00:05:14.856
is as close as any of us
is ever going to get.

00:05:14.939 --> 00:05:17.150
Come on, Charlie.

00:05:17.192 --> 00:05:18.234
I think it’s
time we let

00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:19.944
your grandparents
get some sleep.

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:23.573
Good night, Grandpa George.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:26.201
Night, Charlie.

00:05:26.242 --> 00:05:27.202
Good night, now.

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:28.203
Chair.

00:05:28.286 --> 00:05:29.329
Thank you, dear.

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:32.916
Night, Grandpa Joe.

00:05:34.751 --> 00:05:36.211
Good night, Grandma Georgina.

00:05:36.294 --> 00:05:40.548
Nothing’s impossible, Charlie.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:49.224
♪ ♪

00:05:57.273 --> 00:05:58.107
Good night.

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:01.027
ALL:
Night, Charlie.

00:06:11.246 --> 00:06:13.957
NARRATOR:
Indeed, that very night,

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.377
the impossible had already
been set in motion.

00:06:18.586 --> 00:06:21.297
( wind gusts )

00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:25.510
( engines buzzing )

